(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2608300400-multirotor-pack-solver-and-mass-closure")
  (:adr/title "Endurance is computable, and a drone that does not exist now says so")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-30")
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2608300200-drone-manufacturing-rotor-solver" "ADR-2608136000" "adr-2607102200-kami-render-stack-deps-authority-rename"))
  (:adr/body "# Endurance is computable, and a drone that does not exist now says so

Second and third increments of the drone-manufacturing work started in
adr-2608300200-drone-manufacturing-rotor-solver, which closed the
`thrust -> rotor` gap and named the two that remained.

## What was missing

1. **No battery pack solver.** `kami-engine-echem` is a PEM fuel cell, not a
   lithium pack. Nothing turned a power draw and a duration into a mass, so
   endurance was not computable end to end. `vdesign.powertrain` carries a fixed
   175 Wh/kg figure inside the car designer, which is right for a car and wrong
   for a rotorcraft.
2. **No multirotor mass closure.** `vdesign.physics/close-mass` is car-shaped and
   lives inside an app repo. Nothing could answer \"does this drone exist\".

## Decision

Two more siblings on the existing `cae.solver` contract, both portable zero-dep
`.cljc`.

### `kotoba-lang/kami-engine-pack` (`:rom-pack`)

Cell specific energy scaled to pack level, then derated for the rate the
aircraft actually pulls. An EV cruises near 0.5C where the rate terms are noise;
a multirotor hovers at 3-6C and climbs at 10C or more, where they are not:

- energy: Peukert, delivered capacity falls as `(1/C)^(k-1)`
- power: internal resistance, voltage sags roughly linearly in C

Endurance is `t = DoD * E_nom^k * P^-k`, so **doubling the load leaves
`2^-1.03 = 0.4897` of the endurance, not `0.5`.** A constant-Wh/kg model returns
exactly `0.5` and is optimistic by that gap on every drone it sizes.

The inverse is closed form, not iterated:
`E_nom = (E_required * P^(k-1) / DoD)^(1/k)`. A test bisects on pack mass until
the forward solve returns the target endurance, so the algebra is checked against
something other than itself.

The chemistry table (`:lipo-highrate` `:li-ion-power` `:li-ion-energy` `:lfp`
`:si-anode`) carries cell Wh/kg, max C, Peukert k and sag per C. High-rate cells
trade energy for power; that trade is the reason it is a table. **The figures are
representative catalogue data, not measurements.** An unknown chemistry is
refused rather than defaulted, because a wrong pack mass that looks like a right
one is the expensive failure.

### `kotoba-lang/kami-engine-multirotor`

The mass spiral, over the three sizing solvers. On a rotorcraft THRUST EQUALS
WEIGHT, so every gram is carried by rotors that grow, motors that grow and a pack
that grows. Loop gain is high, and a specification that does not close is
ordinary rather than exotic.

The output is a verdict, not a mass. Divergence IS the refusal — the design-side
counterpart of a safety governor, and the thing that stops a proposer handing
back a 2 kg drone that flies for three hours.

Gates: `:mass-closure`, `:mtow-limit`, `:blade-stall` **at peak thrust**, and
`:pack-rate-hover` / `:pack-rate-peak`. The peak rate gate exists because the
pack is sized by hover and then has to survive the climb: at a 2:1 thrust margin
peak power is `2^1.5 = 2.83x` hover, so the peak C-rate is too. A hover-only
calculation never sees it.

## A defect found and fixed while measuring

The first implementation stopped the iteration when the mass passed the MTOW
ceiling and reported that as `:mass-spiral-runaway`. That conflates two findings
that need different fixes:

```
12 kg payload / 12 min  ->  CLOSES at 26.9 kg   :mtow-limit    a real aircraft, wrong class
1.5 kg payload / 60 min ->  DIVERGES            :mass-closure  no aircraft, any class
```

(These two examples were 10 kg/12 min and 14 kg/12 min when this ADR was
written. Both now CLOSE — see the amendment at the end. The distinction they
illustrate is unchanged; the aircraft that illustrate it are not.)

Before the fix the first was reported as the second — telling an engineer to
abandon a design that merely needed a different class. `close-mass` now ignores
the ceiling entirely (that is regulation, not physics) and `check` applies it as
a gate. Reintroducing the stop reddens exactly the one test written for it.

The initial divergence demo was also misleading for this reason: most of the
\"divergent\" specs were the ceiling firing. Re-measured with the ceiling removed,
they diverge for real, but the demo was not evidence of what it appeared to show.

## A prediction that was wrong, kept because the correction is the result

At fixed disc loading, induced velocity is constant, so hover power is linear in
mass. From that I predicted the loop was affine and closure therefore depended on
endurance and technology but NOT on payload — the same duty would close for every
payload or for none.

Measurement refuted it: 0.1 kg for 30 min closes, 1.5 kg for 30 min does not.
Rotor and motor mass grow as `m^1.5` (blade volume goes as `R^3`; motor mass goes
as `P/omega` with `omega` falling as `1/sqrt(m)`), so the loop is **superlinear**
and closure depends on payload as well. A test pins the pair that refuted the
prediction.

## Evidence

| | tests | assertions | first run |
|---|---|---|---|
| kami-engine-pack | 13 | 57 | green |
| kami-engine-multirotor | 14 | 64 | 2 failures, both my assertions being tighter than the loop's own convergence tolerance rather than defects; they now assert what the algorithm promises |

Both green under `clojure -M:dev:test` and `clojure -M:test` (the git-dep path
the fleet gate takes). clj-kondo 0 errors 0 warnings on both.

Verified DISCRIMINATING by breaking each and confirming the reported failure
matched the break:

| break | result |
|---|---|
| pack: remove the Peukert derating | 13 red |
| pack: treat cell energy as pack energy | 17 red |
| pack: wrong exponent in the closed-form inverse | 5 red, exactly the round-trip and the bisection cross-check |
| multirotor: drop the rotor count from the electrical draw | 21 red |
| multirotor: size the motor from hover instead of peak | 14 red |
| multirotor: reintroduce ceiling-as-divergence | 3 red, exactly the test written for that distinction |

Restoring each returned 0 failures.

### The chain, run

1.5 kg payload, 20 minutes, four rotors, 90 N/m^2, 2:1 thrust margin, high-rate
LiPo:

```
MTOW        5.301 kg
rotors      429 mm at 6821 rpm     blade loading 0.079 at PEAK thrust
hover       600 W electrical       2.48C          peak 1698 W / 7.02C
breakdown   payload 1.500  battery 1.639  structure 1.325  motors 0.458
            avionics 0.250  rotors 0.129
```

A 5.3 kg quadrotor carrying 1.5 kg for 20 minutes on a 1.64 kg pack is a real
airframe of that class, which is the only external check available.

## Also fixed

`kami-engine-rotor` and `kami-engine-pack` had copied kami-engine-motor's
`deps.edn` pins, which were behind their upstream tips (datom by 3 commits,
cae-solver by 92). Both old pins were reachable from main so nothing was broken,
but the workspace rule is that a pin's default state is the upstream tip. Both
advanced; tests re-run green against the newer tips.

## What is still absent

- ~~**No small-motor calibration.**~~ **DONE, and the claim this ADR made about
  it was WRONG IN DIRECTION.** See the amendment at the end.
- **No design actor.** There is no LLM proposer sealed behind this governor, the
  way kami-engine-vehicle-designer seals one behind PhysicsGovernor. The closure
  is the governor; nothing proposes to it yet.
- **No manufacturing or compliance surface.** No BOM, serialisation, remote ID,
  airworthiness or export classification for UAS. `gftdcojp/defence` holds the
  ITAR/ECCN classifier such a vertical would call; they are not wired together.
- **Nothing rotor-to-rotor.** No interference, ground effect, gust margin,
  centre of gravity, packaging or cooling.
- **The fleet gates have never run.** Three `:jvm-test` gates are registered
  (`kami-engine-rotor`, `-pack`, `-multirotor`) and none has been green on the
  fleet yet, so by this workspace's own rule none is landed.


## Amendment, 2026-08-30 — the motor claim was backwards, and fixing it moved the envelope

This ADR asserted that `kami-engine-motor` under-estimates small motors and that
\"every MTOW above is optimistic by the motor error\". **That was asserted from no
data.** Measured the same day against T-MOTOR's own published figures for five
motors (MN2806 / MN4006 / MN5208 / MN8012 / MN8017, 46-453 g, 216-3180 W), the
traction-motor shear model is **40-53% HEAVY** at drone scale, not light:

```
shaft 343 W  ->  traction model 115 g   calibrated  82 g   (+40%)
shaft 800 W  ->  traction model 267 g   calibrated 175 g   (+53%)
```

`motor.bldc` (`:rom-bldc`, in kami-engine-motor) now fits
`mass_g = a * P_W^b` over that table — a = 0.374, b = 0.898, R2 0.979 in log
space, worst residual 18.6%. The fit is computed from the table at call time, so
the constants cannot drift away from their data; the solver reports
`:extrapolated?` outside 216-3180 W and carries `:worst-residual` rather than
inventing confidence. KV is not in the fit (mass tracks torque, and these
datasheets do not state rpm at max power), which is part of that residual.

`multirotor.closure` now calls it. The consequences are not cosmetic:

| | before | after |
|---|---|---|
| baseline MTOW (1.5 kg / 20 min) | 5.301 kg | **4.926 kg** |
| motors (4) | 458 g | **306 g** |
| rotor diameter | 429 mm | **413 mm** |
| hover, electrical | 600 W | **558 W** |
| endurance boundary at 1.5 kg | ~25 min | **~36 min** |
| 14 kg / 12 min | reported DIVERGES | **closes at 31.6 kg** |
| 1.5 kg / 30 min | reported DIVERGES | **closes at 8.66 kg** |

So the aircraft got lighter and **the feasible envelope grew** — designs this ADR
published as non-existent do exist. Two of its own examples were among them.

The general lesson is the one this workspace already writes down and this ADR
still managed to break: **a confident claim about the direction of an error, made
without measuring, is not a caveat — it is a wrong result with a humble tone.**
A refusal is only as good as the sizing beneath it.

Still absent after this amendment: the design actor, the manufacturing and
compliance surface, rotor-to-rotor effects, and any green fleet gate.")))